A||||10||ChadMan||C4 Corvette, or Fiero?||||9
Z||000000||ChadMan||11-09-1999||09:17 AM||||I must confess - I have always loved the C4 Corvettes (84-96) as much as I've loved Fieros.  I always thought when I could afford it, I'd sell all but my trusty daily-driver 88 Coupe and buy a late-80's/early 90's Vette.<P>But I drove one yesterday for the first time - a gorgeous red 89 automatic w/60K miles - and I must admit, I was a little disappointed!  It was faster than my V6 Fiero, but not night-and-day faster like I expected.  The cockpit was really cramped - far more cramped than a Fiero - and much less luggage room than a Fiero, too.  The brakes were great, but not much better than my 88 Coupe.  Handling and steering were the only areas where I thought the Corvette was much better than any sports car/GT I've ever driven.<P>I still love Corvettes, but I'm beginning to think I'd be just as happy or happier if I found a low-mileage 88 GT, or found a loaded 88 Coupe and put a 3800 Buick V6 in it.<P>Deep thoughts? Comments? Snide remarks?||||reg||9
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||11-09-1999||01:22 PM||||I've driven several Corvettes and it never really felt "right". Can't explain it really... When I'm in a Fiero, I get this "This car fits me like a glove" feeling. You forget that you're in a car because it becomes a part of you. The Corvette never gave me that feeling. Don't get me wrong, I don't dislike Corvettes - it's just not my car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlhuber||11-09-1999||01:59 PM||||i too agree.  i've just recently had the chance to get most any reasonable car (by reasonable i mean most anything less thatn a viper), so i set out looking.  i tried different sports/sporty cars such as mustang gt's (88-95), a 97 cobra, 97 probe gt, 99 eclipse gsx, 99 formula ws6/1le, you see where i'm goin.  i also looked at a '90 zr1 'vette, but i took it for a spin and didn't particularly care for it.  yes it was fast, but as it has been said before, it is nothing like drivin' my fiero.  so i've opted to stick with my '86.5 gt and sink some money into her.  i still think the '89 prototype would have sold better then what gm thought, had it actually been built.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Greg Piet||11-09-1999||04:11 PM||||Yeah thats the ticket the 89 Fiero!<BR>Ok we have about 560 members here...  its time for the 560 member march on Pontiac to bring back the Fiero!<BR>Heck they already have the design from the 89 that never was and it still looks pretty sweet by todays standards!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||11-09-1999||04:12 PM||||I've had both. I had an 85 GT automatic that I drove for 7 years. I bought an 84 Corvette with the 4+3 transmission. I sold the Fiero when I bougth the Corvette thinking that I've finally found my dream car. After a couple of months of driving the Corvette I was regreting selling my GT. <P>Granted, the 84 verison of the Corvette had the roughest suspention this side of a 1-ton flat-bed truck and the newer version's ride is totally different. But, the thing that ade driving the Fiero so much fun was how nimble and predictable it was in traffic and on curvy roads. It always seemed that I was wrestling with the Corvette. The Corvette may out perform the Fiero on the track but how much of my "butt" time is going to be spent doing that???||||reg||4
Z||000005||Eric||11-09-1999||07:52 PM||||The autocross tracks are a great example of how a Vette stacks up against a Fiero.  There were a bunch of C4s when I went and not one of them looked like they had a handle of the course.  There were countless spins.  And I could swear, when the drivers steped out of their Corvettes, they were shaking.  I think I was the reason my Fiero couldn't take one of the best times of the event.  My driving style left a lot to be desired, but I still finished at top of C stock and even managed to beat of few of the Vettes consistantly. <P>Those things are strictly high speed machines.  I've only got a little bit of right seat time in Vettes, but I can tell you for the money the Fiero is the way to go.  Not to mention its other great attributes.  I think we've got ourselves one hellava machine, guys.||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||11-09-1999||10:31 PM||||I owned a 95 LT1 6 spd last year with some engine mods it was pushing 360hp. It also had the ZR1 wheels w/ 315-35-17zr's.  I also owned a 87 GT w/ a chip, intake, exhaust, and 17" wheels.  Other than just the brute force of the Vette the Fiero was much more enjoyable to drive.  The handling was close between the two.  It took much more effort and concentration to drive the Vette.  I sometimes miss that power so of course the solution is the new Fiero project I have...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||lowCG||11-10-1999||01:22 AM||||Anyone remember that guy named Zora Duntov,who kept trying to create a mid-engined corvette,after years of heading up the program at GM?<BR>He made only one,so much for progress.<BR>Heck,I'd really love a Pantera,just that I'm about $20K short.<BR>Better keep my Fiero roadracer project going.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||11-10-1999||09:32 AM||||Dont the new Vette's have the Bell housing at  the engine and the rest of the Tranny at the rear end?  It seems like I read an article on the new ones about the split transmission to improve power distribution or something.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||11-10-1999||09:43 AM||||Yep - Porsche 928's and 944's were laid out this way, too.  (So was the 1961-63 Pontiac Tempest!)<P>Now I'm certain the C5 Corvette is superior to the Fiero in every way possible.  But I don't have $45K lying around to spend on a sports car!<P>I still love the C4 Vettes - I just appreciate my Fieros more after driving a C4!  (I didn't even mention the 6" door sill you have to climb over to get into a C4.  Sheesh!)||||reg||1
Z||000010||RZURCTN||11-10-1999||11:11 AM||||928's?  Hey, I've got one of those!    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/rzurctn/Porsche2.jpg">    If Anyone wants to buy it it is for sale.  It needs a lot of work though!  I haven't got to drive it a whole lot, but I still like the way my Fiero handles and feels a lot better.  For it's apparently small size, the 928 feels like a Cadillac.  Maybe it's that long front end.  928's were designed to be fully balanced with a 150 Lb man in the drivers seat.  It's a great car and a marvel of German engineering.  This one is a 1979 European model.  It came over from Spain.  There isn't a whole lot that I can do with it here, it has no emissions on it and I can't get it registered without them.<P>[This message has been edited by RZURCTN (edited 11-10-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by RZURCTN (edited 11-10-1999).]||||reg||6
